+# Approve button: why the label vanishes into one big spinner
+
+## What DaisyUI actually does with `btn ... loading`
+
+In modern DaisyUI (v3+, which is what Scaffold-ETH 2 ships), **`loading` is not a button modifier — it is a standalone spinner component.** The old v1/v2 `.btn.loading` "show a spinner instead of the label" behavior was removed. `loading` is now meant to be its own element: `<span class="loading loading-spinner"></span>`.
+
+The `.loading` class roughly compiles to:
+
+```css
+.loading {
+  pointer-events: none;
+  display: inline-block;
+  aspect-ratio: 1 / 1;              /* force a square */
+  width: 1.5rem;                    /* loading-md default */
+  background-color: currentColor;   /* paint the whole box */
+  -webkit-mask: url("…spinner svg…") center / contain no-repeat;
+          mask: url("…spinner svg…") center / contain no-repeat;
+  /* the mask animates → spinning dots */
+}
+```
+
+So when you write `className="btn btn-primary loading"`, you're not adding a small spinner **to** the button — you're telling the browser to turn **the button element itself into a spinner**:
+
+- **`aspect-ratio: 1/1`** collapses the button to a square. Its nice pill width is gone → **the layout jumps** the moment the tx goes pending.
+- **`background-color: currentColor`** floods the entire button surface with the text color.
+- **`mask: …spinner…`** clips that flood to the spinner shape and animates it. The whole button *is* the spinner now — **it fills the entire button** because the mask covers the element's whole background box.
+- The `"Approving…"` text is still a child node, but it's drawn on a box that has been reshaped to a square and whose surface is a masked, animated spinner. Between the collapsed square, the `currentColor` fill, and the spinner mask sitting over the content area, **the label is visually swallowed / clipped away** — you never read it.
+
+Net effect exactly matches the bug report: square button, one big spinner, no text, layout shift, "looks broken." And it's platform-independent because it's pure CSS on the element — nothing responsive about it.
+
+## The fix — idiomatic DaisyUI / SE-2 pattern
+
+Keep the button a button. Put a **separate** small spinner element **next to** the (still visible) label. `btn` is already `inline-flex` with a gap, so a child spinner + text line up automatically.
+
+```tsx
+<button className="btn btn-primary" disabled={isPending}>
+  {isPending && <span className="loading loading-spinner loading-sm" />}
+  {isPending ? "Approving..." : "Approve"}
+</button>
+```
+
+Why this is correct:
+
+- **`loading loading-spinner loading-sm`** is the standalone spinner, sized small so it sits inline instead of dictating the button's shape.
+- The spinner is a **sibling** of the text, not a class on the button — so the button keeps its normal size, padding, and pill shape. **No layout jump, label stays visible.**
+- `disabled={isPending}` blocks double-submits and gives the built-in disabled styling. (The old `loading` class also set `pointer-events: none`; `disabled` now carries that responsibility.)
+- To fully prevent width shift as the label swaps `Approve` → `Approving...`, you can fix a min-width, e.g. `className="btn btn-primary min-w-32"`.
+
+### Note on DaisyUI v5
+
+Same conclusion. In v5 `loading` is still a standalone component and still not a `btn` modifier, so `btn loading` is still wrong for the same reasons; the fix above is unchanged.

